# North America batch (1497-1819): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"na14970": dict(
 bluf="Venetian navigator John Cabot sailed west for English King Henry VII to find a short route to Asia, and the king wanted a share of what Columbus had opened for Spain. Cabot found Newfoundland and seas full of cod rather than spices. His landfall gave England its legal claim to North America, the basis of every later English colony, and the fishery pulled the North Atlantic into the European economy.",
 now="England's title to North America rested on Cabot's landfall and was revived to justify Newfoundland in 1583 and Jamestown in 1607. The cod fishery built Canada's Atlantic provinces and ran until overfishing forced the moratorium of 1992.",
 story="Venetian navigator John Cabot sailed from Bristol in 1497 under letters patent from English King Henry VII, hoping to reach Asia. He landed on the coast of Newfoundland and found no spices. His crew reported waters so thick with cod that the fish could be scooped up in baskets.",
 why="Columbus's voyage of 1492 had given Spain a western route, and Henry VII wanted England's share. Bristol merchants already fished far out into the Atlantic and could fund a voyage.",
 then="England let the claim lie for most of a century, then revived it to justify Newfoundland in 1583 and Jamestown in 1607. Basque, Portuguese, French and English fleets worked the cod banks, and salt cod became a staple of European trade.",
 fact="Henry VII rewarded Cabot with £10 for finding the 'new isle'."),
"na15341": dict(
 bluf="French navigator Jacques Cartier sailed for French King Francis I to find a passage to Asia and gold, and to deny Spain and Portugal the whole of the Americas. He found neither passage nor gold, and his settlements failed. His voyages gave France its claim to the St Lawrence, the river into the interior of the continent, and that route became the backbone of New France and its fur trade with Indigenous nations.",
 now="Cartier's route gave France its claim to the St Lawrence and the fur trade, and the French-speaking society of Quebec that still governs itself inside Canada descends from it.",
 story="French navigator Jacques Cartier entered the Gulf of St Lawrence in 1534 on a voyage for French King Francis I, looking for a passage to Asia and for gold. On his second voyage, in 1535, he sailed up the river and met the Iroquoians of Stadacona. He recorded their word 'kanata', meaning village, as the name of the land.",
 why="Francis I rejected the Spanish-Portuguese division of the world and wanted France's share of the Americas. He hoped for a Northwest Passage to Asia and for gold.",
 then="Cartier's attempt at settlement in 1541-42 failed. The claim held, and French explorer Samuel de Champlain founded Quebec on the same river in 1608.",
 fact="The name 'Canada' comes from an Iroquoian word for village that Cartier recorded."),
"na15872": dict(
 bluf="English courtier Sir Walter Raleigh, holding a royal patent, planted a colony on Roanoke Island as a base for raiding Spanish shipping and a claim to North American land. About 115 settlers under Governor John White landed in 1587; war with Spain delayed resupply, and when ships returned in 1590 the colony was gone. The failure taught England that a colony needed steady supply, capital and workable relations with Native peoples, the lessons behind the joint-stock company that founded Jamestown.",
 now="Roanoke showed that a colony run as one courtier's venture would starve without steady supply and capital. England's next attempt, Jamestown in 1607, was financed by a joint-stock company with far more money behind it.",
 story="English courtier Sir Walter Raleigh held a royal patent to settle North America. About 115 settlers under Governor John White landed on Roanoke Island in 1587. White sailed to England for supplies and was kept there by the war with Spain. When supply ships returned in 1590 the colony was gone, and the only clue was 'CROATOAN' carved into a post.",
 why="Raleigh wanted a base for raiding Spanish shipping and a claim to North American land. England's war with Spain, including the Armada year of 1588, kept every ship at home.",
 then="War with Spain delayed resupply until 1590, by which time the colony was gone. England's next attempt, Jamestown in 1607, was organised by a joint-stock company with far more capital.",
 myth="The colonists vanished without a trace. The carved word named Croatoan Island and its people, who had been friendly to the English, and the likeliest reading is that survivors joined them.",
 fact="Virginia Dare, the first English child born in America, was born at Roanoke in August 1587 and vanished with the colony."),
"na16073": dict(
 bluf="The Virginia Company of London sent about 100 men to found a colony that would pay its investors in gold and a passage to Asia. They found disease, hunger and the Powhatan Confederacy; most early settlers died, and tobacco made the colony pay from 1612. Jamestown's survival on an export crop, land taken from Native peoples and bound labour that turned to African slavery set the model of the plantation South, and in 1619 it produced English America's first representative assembly.",
 now="Representative government, Native dispossession and slavery, central threads of American history, all appear together in Virginia's first decades.",
 story="In 1607 the Virginia Company of London, a joint-stock venture, sent about 100 men to found Jamestown in the territory of the Powhatan Confederacy. They hoped for gold and found disease, hunger and a powerful Native state. Most of the early settlers died, above all in the 'starving time' of 1609-10. Captain John Smith's discipline held the colony together in 1608-09, and tobacco, planted by colonist John Rolfe from 1612, made it pay.",
 why="England wanted its share of the Americas after Spain's success, and investors hoped for gold and a passage to Asia. The joint-stock company let merchants spread the risk.",
 then="Tobacco exports grew and settlers spread onto Powhatan land; the Powhatan struck back in 1622 and 1644. In 1619 Virginia created the House of Burgesses, the first elected assembly in English America. Labour came first from English indentured servants and increasingly from enslaved Africans.",
 fact="In the 'starving time' winter of 1609-10, about 80 per cent of the colonists died."),
"na16084": dict(
 bluf="French explorer Samuel de Champlain built a post at the narrows of the St Lawrence to control the fur trade for France and its merchant backers. The post held, and New France grew as an empire of trade and Indigenous alliance rather than mass settlement, with about 70,000 settlers by 1760. Britain conquered it in 1759-63, and the French-speaking society it left has survived inside an English-speaking continent.",
 now="Quebec's distinct identity, its language laws and its two independence referendums (1980 and 1995) trace back to this colony.",
 story="French explorer Samuel de Champlain built a trading post in 1608 at a narrow point on the St Lawrence, where the river could be commanded. He allied with the Huron and Algonquin and traded guns and goods for beaver pelts, which Europe wanted for hats.",
 why="France wanted control of the fur trade with Indigenous partners, and the narrows at Quebec commanded the St Lawrence.",
 then="French traders and missionaries followed the rivers to the Great Lakes and the Mississippi, allied with the Wendat (Huron) and Algonquin against the Iroquois. With only about 70,000 settlers by 1760, New France fell to Britain in 1759-63.",
 fact="The name Quebec comes from an Algonquin word for the place where the river narrows."),
"na16195": dict(
 bluf="English privateers who had seized Angolans from a Portuguese slave ship traded about 20 of them to Virginia colonists for food at Point Comfort in 1619, the earliest recorded arrival of Africans in English North America. Their legal status was unfixed, and some gained freedom. Over the following decades Virginia wrote lifelong, hereditary, race-based slavery into law, a system that shaped the economy, Constitution and society of the future United States.",
 now="Virginia's laws of the 1660s made slavery lifelong, hereditary and racial, the template every Southern colony copied; the inequality they built still shapes wealth and politics in the United States.",
 story="English privateers seized Angolans from a Portuguese slave ship bound for Mexico. In August 1619 they traded about 20 of them to Virginia colonists at Point Comfort for food. Virginia law said nothing yet about the status of Africans, and within decades the colony wrote race-based, lifelong slavery into its codes.",
 why="Tobacco planters needed labour. English privateers had captives to sell after seizing them from a Portuguese slave ship bound for Mexico.",
 then="The status of Africans varied at first and some gained freedom. From the 1660s Virginia laws made slavery lifelong and hereditary through the mother (1662) and tied it to race. After Bacon's Rebellion in 1676 planters shifted from indentured servants to enslaved Africans.",
 fact="The ship that brought them, the White Lion, was an English privateer sailing under Dutch letters of marque."),
"na16196": dict(
 bluf="The Virginia Company let its colonists elect representatives to make local laws, to attract settlers to a colony that had nearly died. The assembly met at Jamestown in 1619, the first elected legislature in English America. Colonists grew used to governing themselves through their own assemblies, and taxes imposed by Parliament without their consent in the 1760s looked like tyranny; the same year's arrival of enslaved Africans meant freedom and slavery grew up together.",
 now="A century and a half of self-government through elected assemblies made Parliament's taxes of the 1760s look illegitimate; the Revolution was fought to defend what Virginia began in 1619.",
 story="The Virginia Company of London reformed the colony's government in 1618-19 to attract settlers. In 1619 colonists elected burgesses who met at Jamestown with the governor and his council to make local laws. It was the first legislature of its kind in English America. The vote went to free men only, and later law narrowed it to property owners.",
 why="The Virginia Company offered a measure of self-government to attract settlers after a decade of deaths and losses.",
 then="Every English colony acquired an elected assembly. Colonists grew used to governing themselves, and taxes imposed by Parliament without their consent in the 1760s looked illegitimate.",
 fact="The first assembly met in the church at Jamestown on 30 July 1619 and sat for six days in heat so severe that one burgess died."),
"na16207": dict(
 bluf="English Separatists who had left the Church of England sailed to America to worship their own way. Blown off course to Cape Cod, they signed the Mayflower Compact to make and obey their own laws, and half of them died in the first winter. The small colony survived on Wampanoag help, the Compact became a founding text of government by consent in America, and Plymouth opened New England to the Puritan migration that shaped American religious and civic culture.",
 now="The Compact is the earliest American statement of government by consent, and Plymouth opened New England to the Puritan migration of the 1630s that shaped the region's religious and civic culture.",
 story="The Pilgrims, English Separatists, sailed on the Mayflower in 1620 to worship their own way. Blown off course, they landed at Cape Cod and signed the Mayflower Compact before going ashore, agreeing to make and obey their own laws. They settled at Plymouth. Half died in the first winter, and help from the Wampanoag under sachem Massasoit and the interpreter Squanto kept the rest alive.",
 why="The Pilgrims had separated from the Church of England and faced persecution at home. They had lived in the Dutch city of Leiden and feared losing their children to Dutch ways.",
 then="The alliance with the Wampanoag held for decades. Puritan migration to Massachusetts Bay followed from 1630. King Philip's War in 1675-76 destroyed Native power in southern New England.",
 fact="Squanto (Tisquantum) spoke English because he had been kidnapped and taken to Europe years earlier."),
"na16308": dict(
 bluf="Puritan lawyer John Winthrop led about 1,000 settlers to build a godly community beyond the reach of English King Charles I, under a charter that let their company govern itself in America. The migration was funded, organised and family-based, and it built a literate, town-governed and intensely religious society. Its ideals, from the 'city upon a hill' to the town meeting, and its intolerance, which banished dissenters, both shaped American civic culture.",
 now="New England's town meetings, public schools and Harvard descend from the colony, and the 'city upon a hill' remains the language of American national mission.",
 story="Puritan lawyer John Winthrop led about 1,000 settlers to Massachusetts Bay in 1630 and founded Boston. The colony's charter let its company govern itself in America. The settlers founded Harvard in 1636 and enforced strict religious conformity, banishing the dissenters Roger Williams and Anne Hutchinson.",
 why="Puritans under pressure from English King Charles I and Archbishop William Laud wanted a community run on their own faith. The royal charter of 1629 let the company's government sit in America rather than London.",
 then="About 20,000 English migrated in the 'Great Migration' of the 1630s. Town meetings, schools and Harvard (1636) took root, and minister Roger Williams founded Rhode Island on religious freedom. Expansion brought the Pequot War of 1637 and King Philip's War of 1675-76.",
 fact="Winthrop's 'city upon a hill' comes from a sermon written for the crossing of 1630; Presidents Kennedy and Reagan both quoted it."),
"na16649": dict(
 bluf="English King Charles II wanted the Dutch colony that split his American colonies in two, and granted it to his brother, the Duke of York, before it was taken. An English fleet entered the harbour in 1664 and Dutch Director-General Peter Stuyvesant surrendered without a fight. England gained the Hudson, a superb harbour and an unbroken coast from Maine to the Carolinas, and New York kept the Dutch habit of trade before creed that made it the commercial capital of the continent.",
 now="The seizure gave England the Hudson and a continuous coast from Maine to Carolina, and New York kept the Dutch habit of trade before creed, which made it the commercial capital of the continent.",
 story="England and the Dutch Republic were rivals for trade. In August 1664 an English fleet sent by the Duke of York sailed into the harbour of New Amsterdam. Dutch Director-General Peter Stuyvesant had few defenders and little support from the townspeople and surrendered. The city was renamed New York for the king's brother.",
 why="England and the Dutch Republic were rivals for trade, and the Dutch colony on the Hudson split England's colonies in two.",
 then="England held an unbroken coast from Maine to the Carolinas. The Dutch retook the city in 1673 and gave it up for good at the Treaty of Westminster in 1674.",
 fact="The surrender terms let the Dutch keep their property, religion and language, and Dutch was still spoken in the Hudson valley in the 1800s."),
"na167510": dict(
 bluf="Wampanoag sachem Metacom, called King Philip by the English, led a coalition of Native nations to stop colonists taking their land and imposing English law. The war of 1675-76 burned dozens of towns on both sides and killed, relative to population, a larger share than any later American war; Metacom was killed and many captives were sold into Caribbean slavery. Native independence in southern New England ended, and the pattern of land pressure, war, enslavement and removal repeated across the continent.",
 now="The war ended Native independence in southern New England and set the pattern of land pressure, war and removal that ran across the continent to 1890.",
 story="Colonists in New England had taken land and imposed their law on Native peoples for decades. Wampanoag sachem Metacom, whom the English called King Philip, led a coalition that included the Nipmuc and Narragansett against the colonies from June 1675. Dozens of towns burned. Metacom was killed in August 1676, and many Native captives, his wife and son among them, were sold into slavery in the Caribbean.",
 why="Colonists kept taking land and imposing their law. Native leaders saw their autonomy shrink with each treaty.",
 trig="Plymouth's execution in June 1675 of three Wampanoag men for killing John Sassamon, a Christian Native informer.",
 then="Dozens of towns burned on both sides. Metacom was killed and many captives were sold into slavery in the Caribbean. The Native nations of southern New England never recovered their independence.",
 fact="Metacom's head was displayed on a pike at Plymouth for some twenty years."),
"na167611": dict(
 bluf="Planter Nathaniel Bacon led frontier settlers, indentured servants and enslaved people against Virginia Governor William Berkeley, demanding war on Native peoples and land for themselves. They burned Jamestown in 1676, and the revolt collapsed when Bacon died of dysentery. Frightened planters shifted from indentured servants to enslaved Africans and passed laws that gave poor whites privileges over Black people, the legal beginning of a racial order that divided workers by colour.",
 now="The laws passed after the revolt gave poor whites privileges over Black people and tied slavery to race, the legal foundation of the racial hierarchy that governed the South until 1865 and shaped it after.",
 story="Tobacco prices were low and Virginia Governor William Berkeley controlled land grants and the Native trade. In 1676 planter Nathaniel Bacon led frontier settlers, servants and enslaved people in attacks on Native peoples and then against the governor. They burned Jamestown in September 1676. Bacon died of dysentery in October and the revolt collapsed.",
 why="Frontier settlers wanted land and war on Native peoples. They resented a governor and elite who controlled land and the Native trade, at a time of low tobacco prices.",
 then="Berkeley hanged 23 rebels. Planters shifted from indentured servants to enslaved Africans and passed laws giving poor whites privileges over Black people, dividing workers by race.",
 fact="English King Charles II is reported to have said that Berkeley had hanged more men in that naked country than he had for the murder of his father."),
"na168212": dict(
 bluf="Quaker William Penn wanted a refuge for persecuted Quakers and a 'holy experiment' in religious tolerance; English King Charles II paid a debt owed to Penn's father with the land in 1681. The colony drew Germans, Scots-Irish and others and became the most diverse in English America. Religious freedom and pluralism became American ideals, and Philadelphia, Penn's planned capital, hosted the Declaration of Independence and the Constitutional Convention.",
 now="Religious freedom and pluralism became core American ideals, and Philadelphia hosted both the Declaration of Independence and the Constitutional Convention.",
 story="English King Charles II owed Admiral William Penn money and paid his son, Quaker William Penn, with a grant of land in 1681. Penn designed Pennsylvania as a 'holy experiment' with religious tolerance and fair dealing with the Lenape, and arrived in 1682. Philadelphia was laid out on a grid.",
 why="Charles II repaid a debt to Penn's father with land. Penn wanted a refuge for Quakers and a 'holy experiment' in tolerance.",
 then="Pennsylvania drew Germans, Scots-Irish and many others and became the most diverse colony. Penn's sons undermined his fair dealing with the Lenape, notably in the fraudulent Walking Purchase of 1737.",
 fact="French writer Voltaire called Penn's treaty with the Lenape the only treaty never sworn to and never broken."),
"na169213": dict(
 bluf="Puritan magistrates in Massachusetts set out to purge Salem Village of witchcraft after girls there had fits in 1692. A special court accepted 'spectral evidence' of dreams and visions, and 20 people were executed before Governor William Phips stopped the trials. The collapse discredited spectral evidence and pushed colonial courts toward stricter standards of proof, and 'witch hunt' became the name for every panic that convicts on fear.",
 now="The trials' collapse discredited spectral evidence and pushed colonial courts toward stricter proof; 'witch hunt' became the term for a panic that convicts on accusation, from the McCarthy hearings of the 1950s to today.",
 story="Girls in Salem Village had strange fits early in 1692 and accusations of witchcraft spread through the surrounding towns. A special court under Massachusetts magistrates accepted 'spectral evidence' of dreams and visions. Nineteen people were hanged and one man was pressed to death before Governor William Phips halted the court in October 1692.",
 why="Local feuds in Salem Village came first. Frontier war with Native peoples and France had filled the region with refugees and fear, and Puritan doctrine made the Devil a daily presence.",
 then="Massachusetts courts stopped accepting spectral evidence. The colony later apologised and in 1711 paid compensation to the victims' families.",
 fact="Nobody was burned. Nineteen were hanged and one man, Giles Corey, was pressed to death under stones."),
"na173914": dict(
 bluf="About 60 enslaved people, many from Kongo with military experience, seized weapons near Charleston in September 1739 and marched for Spanish Florida, which promised freedom to runaways from the British colonies. Militia caught and killed most of them within days. It was the largest slave revolt in Britain's mainland colonies, and South Carolina answered it with the Negro Act of 1740, the pattern by which slave societies met resistance with tighter control.",
 now="Stono shows that enslaved Africans resisted from the start, and that the slave codes which defined Southern law were written in response to that resistance.",
 story="Spanish Florida had offered freedom to enslaved people who fled the British colonies. On 9 September 1739 about 60 enslaved people, many from Kongo with military experience, seized weapons from a store near the Stono River and headed south for Florida. South Carolina militia caught and killed most of them.",
 why="Brutal conditions on the rice plantations came first. Spanish Florida's offer of freedom to runaways, a deliberate tactic against Britain, gave the rebels a destination.",
 then="South Carolina's Negro Act of 1740 barred enslaved people from gathering, growing their own food, earning money or learning to read.",
 fact="Kongo had been a Catholic kingdom since 1491, which is one reason Catholic Spanish Florida drew the rebels."),
"na175415": dict(
 bluf="Britain and France both claimed the Ohio valley, and France built forts there to link Canada with Louisiana. A Virginia militia party under Lieutenant Colonel George Washington attacked a French detachment in May 1754, and the clash spread into the Seven Years' War across Europe, India and the oceans. Britain won and removed France from North America, and the debt it ran up produced the colonial taxes that led to the American Revolution.",
 now="Britain's war debt produced the Stamp Act of 1765 and the taxes that drove the colonies to revolt, and the removal of France removed the colonists' need for British protection.",
 story="Britain and France both claimed the Ohio River Valley, and the French built forts there in 1753-54. In May 1754 Virginia militia under Lieutenant Colonel George Washington attacked a French party at Jumonville Glen. The fighting spread to Europe, India and the oceans as the Seven Years' War, which Britain declared in 1756.",
 why="Both Britain and France claimed the Ohio Valley. The French built forts there to link Canada with Louisiana, and Virginia land speculators wanted the same ground.",
 trig="Washington's attack on a French party at Jumonville Glen in May 1754.",
 then="Britain won and took Canada at the Treaty of Paris in 1763. Its war debt led Parliament to tax the colonies. Native nations lost their ability to play the two empires against each other.",
 fact="Washington surrendered Fort Necessity to the French on 4 July 1754, the only surrender of his career."),
"na176316": dict(
 bluf="Britain wanted its victory in the Seven Years' War turned into territory; France wanted peace and kept its sugar islands. France handed Canada and the land east of the Mississippi to Britain and Louisiana to Spain, and Britain dominated North America from Hudson Bay to Florida. The removal of the French threat removed the colonists' main reason to depend on Britain, and the cost of the new empire produced the taxes that drove them to revolt.",
 now="Britain's victory produced both Canada's British identity and the American Revolution: the colonies needed Britain less, and Britain taxed them more to pay for the empire it had won.",
 story="Defeated in the Seven Years' War, France signed the Treaty of Paris on 10 February 1763. France ceded Canada and the land east of the Mississippi to Britain and had already given Louisiana to Spain. Spain ceded Florida to Britain. France kept Guadeloupe and Martinique. Britain dominated the continent's east.",
 why="Britain had won the war in North America, India and at sea. France gave up its North American empire to make peace and kept the sugar islands that earned more.",
 then="Free of the French threat, colonists needed British protection much less. Britain's Proclamation of 1763, meant to calm Native nations after Pontiac's War, barred settlement west of the Appalachians and angered colonists.",
 fact="French writer Voltaire dismissed Canada as 'a few acres of snow'."),
"na176517": dict(
 bluf="Parliament under Prime Minister George Grenville taxed newspapers, legal documents and playing cards in the colonies in 1765 to pay for the troops kept in America after the war. Colonists boycotted British goods, rioted and met in a Stamp Act Congress, and Parliament repealed the act in 1766 while asserting its right to legislate 'in all cases whatsoever'. The first direct tax produced the first united colonial resistance and the principle that taxation requires consent.",
 now="The Stamp Act produced the first united colonial resistance and the principle that taxation requires the consent of the taxed; its repeal taught the colonies that resistance worked.",
 story="To pay war debts and the cost of troops in America, Parliament under Prime Minister George Grenville passed the Stamp Act in March 1765, taxing newspapers, legal documents and playing cards in the colonies. Colonists boycotted British goods, crowds attacked stamp distributors, and nine colonies sent delegates to a Stamp Act Congress in New York in October 1765. Parliament repealed the act in March 1766.",
 why="Britain needed to pay its war debt and the cost of keeping troops in America.",
 then="Parliament repealed the act a year later and passed the Declaratory Act, asserting its right to legislate for the colonies 'in all cases whatsoever'. Colonists learned that united protest worked, and Britain kept trying new taxes.",
 fact="The Sons of Liberty took their name from a speech in Parliament by Colonel Isaac Barré defending the colonists."),
"na177018": dict(
 bluf="British troops had been stationed in Boston since 1768 to enforce customs duties the town resisted. On 5 March 1770 soldiers facing a crowd throwing snowballs and rocks fired and killed five men, among them Crispus Attucks, a man of African and Native descent. Patriots turned the killings into their most powerful propaganda through silversmith Paul Revere's engraving, and the acquittal of most of the soldiers, defended by lawyer John Adams, showed the colonists' claim to the rule of law.",
 now="Revere's engraving showed how one image can fix a political cause, and the annual massacre orations kept Boston's anger alive until the war in 1775.",
 story="British troops had been in Boston since 1768 to enforce customs duties. On 5 March 1770 a crowd pelted a sentry and the soldiers who came to his aid with snowballs and rocks; the soldiers fired, killing five, including Crispus Attucks, a man of African and Native descent. Silversmith Paul Revere's engraving of the scene spread the story through the colonies.",
 why="Tension over British troops stationed in Boston to enforce unpopular customs duties.",
 then="Lawyer John Adams defended the soldiers in court; most were acquitted and two were convicted of manslaughter. Patriots marked the anniversary each year with speeches that kept anger alive until the war.",
 fact="Revere's engraving was copied from a drawing by artist Henry Pelham, who complained of the theft."),
"na177319": dict(
 bluf="The Sons of Liberty set out to stop East India Company tea landing in Boston under the Tea Act, which gave the company a monopoly and carried Parliament's tax. On 16 December 1773 men disguised as Mohawks boarded three ships and destroyed 342 chests of tea. Britain answered with the Coercive Acts, closing the port and taking over Massachusetts' government, which united the colonies in the First Continental Congress in 1774 and led within eighteen months to war.",
 now="Britain's response, the Coercive Acts of 1774, turned a Massachusetts dispute into a continental one; the First Continental Congress met within months.",
 story="The Tea Act of 1773 gave the East India Company a monopoly on tea in the colonies and kept Parliament's tax on it. Three tea ships lay in Boston harbour and the governor refused to let them leave unloaded. On the night of 16 December 1773 the Sons of Liberty, disguised as Mohawks, boarded the ships and dumped 342 chests of tea into the harbour.",
 why="Protest against the Tea Act, which gave the East India Company a monopoly in the colonies and reasserted Parliament's right to tax them.",
 then="Britain closed Boston's port and took over Massachusetts' government, pushing the colonies to unite in the First Continental Congress (1774).",
 fact="The East India Company tea they dumped would be worth over $1 million today."),
"d-bunker": dict(
 bluf="Massachusetts militia under Colonel William Prescott fortified Breed's Hill to threaten the British in besieged Boston; British Major General William Howe attacked to clear it. On 17 June 1775 three frontal assaults took the position when the defenders ran out of powder, at over 1,000 British casualties to about 450 American. The cost made British commanders cautious for the rest of the war and gave the Americans confidence that militia could stand against regulars.",
 now="Bunker Hill showed that the Revolution would be a war rather than a riot, and that Britain faced a long and costly fight.",
 story="Militia under Colonel William Prescott fortified Breed's Hill overlooking Boston on the night of 16 June 1775. On 17 June British troops under Major General William Howe made three frontal assaults. They took the position when the defenders ran out of powder, at a cost of over 1,000 British casualties to about 450 American.",
 why="Both sides wanted the heights around besieged Boston, from which guns could command the town and the harbour.",
 then="The British left Boston in March 1776 after General George Washington placed cannon on Dorchester Heights.",
 myth="'Don't fire until you see the whites of their eyes' was coined at Bunker Hill. The order was a commonplace of 18th-century war, and the evidence that anyone gave it there is thin.",
 fact="Most of the fighting was on Breed's Hill; the battle took its name from the hill the militia had been ordered to fortify."),
"na177520": dict(
 bluf="British General Thomas Gage sent about 700 regulars to seize militia weapons and powder stored at Concord. Riders including silversmith Paul Revere gave warning, militia met the column at Lexington and Concord on 19 April 1775 and harried it back to Boston. The first shots turned a political dispute into armed rebellion: within weeks New England militia had Boston under siege and Congress had created an army.",
 now="The shots turned a political dispute into armed rebellion; Boston was under siege within weeks and the Continental Army existed within two months.",
 story="British General Thomas Gage ordered about 700 regulars to march from Boston to seize colonial weapons and powder stored at Concord. Riders including silversmith Paul Revere warned the countryside. Militia met the column on Lexington green at dawn on 19 April 1775, where eight militiamen were killed, and at Concord's North Bridge, then harried the British all the way back to Boston.",
 why="British troops tried to seize colonial weapons and powder stored at Concord.",
 then="Within weeks colonial forces had Boston under siege. The Continental Congress formed the Continental Army under General George Washington in June 1775.",
 fact="19 April is still Patriots' Day in Massachusetts, the day of the Boston Marathon."),
"d-commonsense": dict(
 bluf="English-born writer Thomas Paine wrote to persuade colonists who still hoped for reconciliation with King George III that independence and a republic were the only sense. 'Common Sense' sold perhaps 100,000 copies or more in its first year, in colonies of about 2.5 million people, in language ordinary readers could follow. Six months after it appeared Congress declared independence, and the pamphlet showed what mass print persuasion could do.",
 now="It is a model of political persuasion aimed at a mass audience, the ancestor of the modern pamphlet and opinion column.",
 story="Fighting had begun in April 1775 and many colonists still hoped for peace with the king. English-born writer Thomas Paine published 'Common Sense' anonymously in Philadelphia in January 1776. It attacked monarchy itself and argued that an island was unfit to rule a continent. It sold perhaps 100,000 copies or more in its first year, in colonies of about 2.5 million people.",
 why="Fighting had begun, and many colonists still hoped to make peace with the king. Paine had arrived from England in late 1774 with an introduction from Benjamin Franklin and no loyalty to the crown.",
 then="Six months later Congress declared independence, using many of Paine's arguments.",
 fact="Paine had failed as a corset-maker, exciseman and shopkeeper before he arrived in America in 1774."),
"na177621": dict(
 bluf="The Continental Congress declared independence to turn a war already fifteen months old into a cause foreign powers could back, since France would arm a sovereign nation rather than rebels seeking better terms. The Declaration, drafted by Virginian Thomas Jefferson and adopted on 4 July 1776, justified the break by universal rights and government by consent. France allied with the United States in 1778, and the claim that 'all men are created equal' clashed from the start with slavery, a contradiction that has driven American politics since.",
 now="Its words inspired revolutions worldwide and were used by abolitionists, suffragists and civil rights leaders to demand that America live up to them.",
 story="Fifteen months into the war, on 4 July 1776, the Continental Congress adopted Virginian Thomas Jefferson's draft announcing independence. It argued that governments get their power from the consent of the governed and that people may overthrow tyrants, then listed grievances against King George III. Congress cut a passage condemning the slave trade from the draft; Jefferson himself enslaved hundreds of people over his life.",
 why="War had begun, and independence was needed to win foreign allies, especially France, which would back a new nation rather than rebels seeking better terms.",
 then="France saw a cause worth backing and joined the war in 1778, and the Declaration's language of universal rights was echoed in France's Declaration of the Rights of Man in 1789.",
 fact="John Adams and Thomas Jefferson, rivals and friends, both died on 4 July 1826, the 50th anniversary."),
"d-saratoga": dict(
 bluf="British General John Burgoyne marched south from Canada to cut New England off from the other colonies along the Hudson, and American General Horatio Gates, with Benedict Arnold leading in the field, set out to stop him. Burgoyne's army was surrounded and surrendered on 17 October 1777. The loss of a whole British army persuaded France to enter the war in 1778, which turned a colonial rebellion into a global war Britain could not win.",
 now="Saratoga brought France into the war in February 1778, and with it the money, navy and troops that won Yorktown; it is the strategic turning point of the Revolution.",
 story="British General John Burgoyne marched south from Canada in June 1777 with about 8,000 men to cut New England off along the Hudson. American General Horatio Gates's army, with Benedict Arnold leading in the field, fought him at Freeman's Farm on 19 September and Bemis Heights on 7 October. Burgoyne's army was surrounded and surrendered on 17 October 1777.",
 why="Britain planned to cut New England off along the Hudson, and poor coordination between British commanders left Burgoyne without support; General William Howe took his army to Philadelphia instead.",
 then="France signed an alliance with the United States in February 1778, and Spain and the Dutch Republic later joined the war, stretching British forces around the world.",
 fact="Arnold, wounded in the leg at Bemis Heights, was the hero of the battle; after his treason in 1780 the monument there shows a boot and no name."),
"na178122": dict(
 bluf="General George Washington and French General Rochambeau marched south to trap British General Charles Cornwallis's army at Yorktown while French Admiral de Grasse's fleet closed the Chesapeake. Cornwallis surrendered over 7,000 troops on 19 October 1781 after a three-week siege. Sea power decided it: Britain could neither supply nor rescue an army it could not protect at sea, and the defeat brought down Lord North's government and ended the war in all but name.",
 now="American independence was won with French troops, French money and a French fleet; the alliance of 1778 decided the war.",
 story="British General Charles Cornwallis fortified Yorktown on the Virginia coast in 1781. General George Washington and French General Rochambeau marched their armies south from New York while French Admiral de Grasse's fleet beat off the British navy at the Chesapeake Capes in September and blocked escape by sea. Cornwallis surrendered over 7,000 troops on 19 October after a three-week siege.",
 why="A French fleet under Admiral de Grasse blocked the Chesapeake, trapping Cornwallis's army against the sea.",
 then="Lord North's government fell in March 1782, and peace talks led to the Treaty of Paris in 1783.",
 fact="Cornwallis pleaded illness and sent a deputy to surrender his sword; Washington had a deputy of his own receive it."),
"na178323": dict(
 bluf="American envoys Benjamin Franklin, John Adams and John Jay wanted recognition and the widest possible borders; Britain wanted out of a costly war and the United States pulled away from France. The treaty signed on 3 September 1783 recognised American independence and set the western border at the Mississippi, roughly doubling the area of the former colonies. The first modern state born from a colonial revolt had room to expand west, at the expense of Native nations who were never consulted.",
 now="The treaty roughly doubled the area of the former colonies and opened the way west, onto the land of Native nations the treaty ignored.",
 story="Signed in Paris on 3 September 1783 by American envoys Benjamin Franklin, John Adams and John Jay and British negotiator David Hartley, the treaty recognised the independence of the United States and set its western border at the Mississippi River. Britain kept Canada and returned Florida to Spain in a separate treaty.",
 why="Britain wanted to end a costly war, and to pull the United States away from France with generous terms.",
 then="The new nation was free but weak, deeply in debt and loosely governed under the Articles of Confederation, problems that led to the Constitution four years later.",
 fact="The Americans negotiated separately from France, against Congress's instructions, and got better terms for it."),
"d-shays": dict(
 bluf="Farmers of western Massachusetts led by war veteran Daniel Shays shut down county courts in 1786 to stop foreclosures, squeezed by state taxes and debts payable in scarce hard money. A privately funded state militia scattered them at the Springfield arsenal in January 1787. The revolt convinced the propertied leadership that the Articles of Confederation could neither keep order nor pay debts, and the Philadelphia convention met within the year.",
 now="The rebellion convinced the propertied leadership that the Confederation could not keep order, and it produced the Philadelphia convention within a year; the Constitution's strong federal government is its direct result.",
 story="Squeezed by state taxes and debts payable in scarce hard money, farmers in western Massachusetts led by war veteran Daniel Shays shut down county courts in 1786 to stop foreclosures. In January 1787 a state militia funded by Boston merchants scattered them at the Springfield arsenal.",
 why="A post-war depression and heavy state taxes to pay off war debts drove the farmers. The Confederation Congress had neither money nor an army to send.",
 then="The revolt helped persuade the states to send delegates to the Philadelphia Convention in May 1787.",
 fact="Thomas Jefferson, then American minister in Paris, wrote that 'a little rebellion now and then is a good thing'."),
"na178724": dict(
 bluf="Delegates led by Virginian James Madison, General George Washington and New Yorker Alexander Hamilton met in Philadelphia in 1787 to replace a Confederation that could neither tax, regulate trade nor put down revolt. They produced a federal republic with separated powers, checks and balances and a national government able to tax, and kept the South in by counting enslaved people as three-fifths of a person and protecting the slave trade until 1808. It is the oldest written national constitution still in force, and its compromises set the terms of American politics for two centuries.",
 now="It is the oldest written national constitution still in use. Debates over the Electoral College, the Senate's small-state advantage and federal versus state power are rooted in its compromises.",
 story="The Articles of Confederation left Congress unable to tax, regulate trade or enforce laws. From May to September 1787, 55 delegates in Philadelphia, among them Virginian James Madison, New Yorker Alexander Hamilton and General George Washington presiding, wrote a new framework: a two-chamber Congress (the 'Great Compromise' between large and small states), an elected president, an independent judiciary and federal supremacy over the states. To keep the South in, they counted enslaved people as three-fifths of a person for representation and protected the slave trade until 1808.",
 why="Under the Articles of Confederation the national government could neither tax, pay its war debts, regulate trade between states nor put down revolts such as Shays' Rebellion.",
 then="After a fierce ratification fight that produced the Federalist Papers, it took effect in 1789, and the Bill of Rights followed in 1791.",
 fact="Rhode Island refused to send anyone to the convention."),
"d-washington": dict(
 bluf="The states had ratified a Constitution that sketched a presidency without defining it, and the one man trusted by every faction, General George Washington, took the office to make it work. He set precedents the text did not require: a cabinet of department heads, the plain title 'Mr President' and a voluntary departure after two terms. The republic learned that power would rotate, and the two-term rule held until 1940 and became law in 1951.",
 now="Washington's departure after two terms made rotation of power the norm; the rule held until Franklin Roosevelt broke it in 1940, and the 22nd Amendment of 1951 wrote it into the Constitution.",
 story="On 30 April 1789 General George Washington swore the oath on the balcony of Federal Hall in New York, then the capital. He set lasting precedents, from the simple title 'Mr President' to forming a cabinet of department heads.",
 why="Washington was the one figure trusted by every faction, and his record of giving up power made it safe to create a strong presidency.",
 then="He stepped down after two terms in 1797, setting a two-term tradition that held until 1940.",
 fact="The electoral college chose Washington unanimously in 1789 and again in 1792, the only president so elected."),
"na179125": dict(
 bluf="Anti-Federalists feared the new central government, and supporters of the Constitution had promised amendments to win ratification. Virginian James Madison drafted them in 1789 and the states ratified ten in 1791, protecting speech, religion, the press, arms and fair trials as limits on federal power. At first the limits bound only the federal government; after the 14th Amendment courts applied them to the states, mostly in the 20th century, and they became the core of American civil liberties.",
 now="Free speech, gun rights and police searches are still argued in Bill of Rights terms, and the 14th Amendment of 1868 let courts apply the first ten amendments to the states.",
 story="Opponents of the Constitution feared a powerful central government, and several states ratified only on the promise of amendments. Virginian James Madison introduced amendments in the first Congress in 1789. Ten were ratified by December 1791, protecting speech, religion, the press, the right to bear arms and fair trials.",
 why="Supporters of the Constitution promised amendments to win over Anti-Federalists who feared central power.",
 then="At first it limited only the federal government; after the Civil War the 14th Amendment let courts apply it to the states, mostly during the 20th century.",
 fact="Twelve amendments went to the states; one of the two left unratified, on congressional pay, was finally adopted in 1992 as the 27th Amendment."),
"na179326": dict(
 bluf="Inventor Eli Whitney, working on a Georgia plantation, built a machine in 1793 to pull the seeds from short-staple cotton, which paid only if the seeds could be removed cheaply. The gin worked, and short-staple cotton became profitable across the inland South. Planters pushed west with enslaved workers; enslaved people in the United States grew from about 700,000 in 1790 to nearly 4 million by 1860, and cotton became the leading export, tying Southern slavery to Britain's textile mills.",
 now="The gin revived slavery when it might have declined and bound the South's economy to Britain's mills; the enslaved population rose from about 700,000 in 1790 to nearly 4 million by 1860.",
 story="Inventor Eli Whitney's gin pulled seeds from cotton far faster than hands could. Growing short-staple cotton suddenly paid almost anywhere in the South, and planters pushed west into the Deep South with enslaved workers.",
 why="Short-staple cotton, which grew across the inland South, paid only if its sticky seeds could be removed cheaply, and Britain's mills wanted raw cotton.",
 then="Enslaved people in the US grew from about 700,000 in 1790 to nearly 4 million by 1860. Cotton became the country's leading export.",
 fact="Whitney made little money from the gin. He got rich making muskets with interchangeable parts."),
"d-marbury": dict(
 bluf="Chief Justice John Marshall, a Federalist, faced a suit by William Marbury against Secretary of State James Madison for a judgeship the outgoing administration had promised him. Marshall ruled on 24 February 1803 that Marbury had a right to the post and that the law letting him sue in the Supreme Court was unconstitutional and void. The Court had struck down an act of Congress, which established judicial review and made the judiciary the final interpreter of the Constitution.",
 now="Judicial review made the Supreme Court the final interpreter of the Constitution, a power that courts in many countries have since copied.",
 story="Outgoing President John Adams had appointed William Marbury a justice of the peace, and the new administration of President Thomas Jefferson withheld his commission. Marbury sued Secretary of State James Madison in the Supreme Court. Chief Justice John Marshall ruled on 24 February 1803 that Marbury was entitled to the commission, and that the law letting him sue directly in the Supreme Court was unconstitutional and therefore void.",
 why="A political fight between the outgoing Federalists, who had filled the courts with their own men, and President Thomas Jefferson's Republicans.",
 then="Judicial review became a pillar of American government, though the Court struck down no further federal law until the Dred Scott case in 1857.",
 fact="Marshall had been the secretary of state who failed to deliver Marbury's commission in the first place."),
"na180327": dict(
 bluf="President Thomas Jefferson sent envoys to buy New Orleans, through which western farmers shipped their crops. French ruler Napoleon Bonaparte, his army destroyed in Haiti and needing money for war with Britain, sold the whole of Louisiana: 828,000 square miles for about $15 million. The United States doubled in size and became a continental power, and the question of slavery in the new territories grew over the next half-century into civil war.",
 now="Fifteen of today's states lie partly or wholly in the territory, and the Mississippi basin it secured became the agricultural heart of the country.",
 story="French ruler Napoleon Bonaparte needed money after his army was destroyed in Haiti and gave up on an American empire. President Thomas Jefferson's envoys Robert Livingston and James Monroe, sent to buy New Orleans, signed in April 1803 for 828,000 square miles.",
 why="Napoleon needed money for war with Britain and had given up on an American empire after his army was destroyed in Haiti. Jefferson wanted New Orleans, through which western farmers shipped their crops.",
 then="It opened the West to settlement and set off decades of fights over whether new states would allow slavery. The Native nations who lived there were never consulted, and their lands were taken over the following century.",
 fact="The US paid about 3 cents an acre."),
"na180428": dict(
 bluf="President Thomas Jefferson sent Captains Meriwether Lewis and William Clark to explore the Louisiana territory, find a water route to the Pacific and stake a claim to the Northwest before Britain. The Corps of Discovery reached the Pacific in 1805 and returned in 1806 with maps and scientific notes, helped by Native nations and by Sacagawea, a Shoshone woman. The journey strengthened US claims to the Oregon Country and set the pattern of the West: exploration and trade, then settlement that dispossessed the nations whose help made it possible.",
 now="It set the pattern of the American West: exploration and trade followed by settlement, which dispossessed the Native nations whose help made the expedition possible.",
 story="President Thomas Jefferson sent Captains Meriwether Lewis and William Clark up the Missouri in 1804 to explore the new territory. Guided in part by Sacagawea, a Shoshone woman, the Corps of Discovery reached the Pacific in November 1805 and returned to St Louis in September 1806 with maps and scientific notes.",
 why="Jefferson wanted scientific knowledge, a water route to the Pacific for the fur trade, and a claim to the Northwest before Britain made one.",
 then="Their journey strengthened US claims to the Oregon Country, and fur traders and later settlers followed their route west.",
 fact="Only one member died in over two years, probably of appendicitis."),
"na180829": dict(
 bluf="Congress banned the importing of enslaved people from 1 January 1808, the first day the Constitution allowed, with President Thomas Jefferson's support. Slavery itself kept growing: smuggling continued, and the internal trade from the Upper South to the cotton states sold around a million people over the following decades, tearing families apart. The ban raised the value of the people already enslaved, which is why many slaveholders backed it.",
 now="The ban made the domestic trade the supply of the cotton states; around a million people were sold south from the Upper South before 1860, the 'second middle passage' inside the United States.",
 story="The Constitution barred Congress from ending the slave trade before 1808. Congress passed a ban in March 1807, with President Thomas Jefferson's support, to take effect on 1 January 1808, the first possible day. Smuggling continued, and the domestic slave trade grew in its place.",
 why="The Constitution barred a ban before 1808; by then even many slaveholders supported one, since it raised the value of the people they already enslaved and eased fears of revolt after Haiti.",
 then="Around a million enslaved people were sold from the Upper South to the Deep South, often tearing families apart.",
 fact="Britain banned its own slave trade in the same year, 1807."),
"na181230": dict(
 bluf="The United States under President James Madison declared war on Britain in June 1812 over the seizure of American sailors, limits on its trade and British arms to Native resistance, and hoped to conquer Canada. The invasions failed, Britain burned the White House in 1814 and failed at Baltimore, and the Treaty of Ghent restored the pre-war borders. The stalemate confirmed American independence, broke Native resistance in the Old Northwest with Shawnee leader Tecumseh's death, and gave Canada a founding story of resisting the United States.",
 now="It confirmed American independence and gave Canada a story of resisting the United States, one root of its distinct identity.",
 story="Britain was seizing American sailors and blocking US trade with France. The United States under President James Madison declared war in June 1812 and tried to invade Canada; the invasions failed. Shawnee leader Tecumseh, allied with Britain, was killed at the Thames in October 1813. British troops burned the White House in August 1814 and failed to take Baltimore in September. The Treaty of Ghent, signed in December 1814, restored the pre-war borders.",
 why="Britain was seizing American sailors and blocking US trade with France, and many Americans blamed Britain for arming Native resistance and hoped to conquer Canada.",
 then="The war ended in a draw. Native resistance in the Midwest was crushed with Tecumseh's death, and the Federalist party, which had opposed the war, collapsed.",
 fact="The Battle of New Orleans was fought after the peace treaty had been signed, because the news had yet to cross the Atlantic. The bombardment of Fort McHenry inspired 'The Star-Spangled Banner'."),
"na181931": dict(
 bluf="Secretary of State John Quincy Adams wanted Florida and a defined western border; Spain, weakened by the Napoleonic Wars and revolts across its American empire, could defend neither. General Andrew Jackson's raids against the Seminole had already occupied much of Florida when Spain ceded it in the Adams-Onís Treaty of 1819 and fixed a border running to the Pacific. Military pressure followed by purchase from a weakened power became the method of US expansion, and the border gave the United States its first legal claim to the Pacific coast.",
 now="Adams-Onís gave the United States a border to the Pacific and a method, military pressure then purchase from a weakened power, that it repeated through the century.",
 story="General Andrew Jackson's troops chased Seminole raiders into Spanish Florida in 1818 and seized Spanish forts there. Spain, weak and distracted, ceded Florida in the Adams-Onís Treaty, negotiated by Secretary of State John Quincy Adams and Spanish minister Luis de Onís and signed in February 1819. The treaty also fixed a border between the two powers running to the Pacific.",
 why="Spain, weakened by the Napoleonic Wars and independence revolts, could not defend Florida, and Andrew Jackson's raids against the Seminole had already occupied much of it.",
 then="Florida became a slave territory in 1821 and a state in 1845. The US Army fought long wars to remove the Seminole, many of whom refused to leave.",
 fact="The United States paid Spain nothing; it assumed up to $5 million in claims by its own citizens against Spain."),
}
